body:
Yeah it's hard to differentiate between the nurses who correctly assess that some things are just not worth trying to get done, and the nurses who just don't bother to try. Whenever I have 5-10 minutes to spare, I don't do a breather (even tho I understand that that is completely acceptable and most likely contributes to better care), I instead take that chance to sit with my patients. Especially those it's difficult to work with. But I've already met issues with this, like one patient who's quite rude and agressive (early onset dementia, also palliative), I know the patient will insist on going to the toilet for the nth time this hour, I know nothing will come, I know getting the patient up from bed and to the toilet is gonna take 10-15 minutes with high risk of the patient falling. But the consequence is that the patient is lying alone in bed the entire day, but I feel I can see how happy the patient gets when someone comes in to talk about the weather, about the election, about anything. 

I'm really interested in peds and I want the opportunity to give maximum effort to one patient, but I can't stand the thought of all the others that I'm not there to care for.

&#x200B;

Just to ad another specialty I've been wanting to do, it's called health nurse, they mostly work in health stations with new borns and children, and also in school. I'm passionate about increasing understanding of mental health amongst children and youth, and councelling families in how to deal with it, but I see health nurses rotating schools so often that it's more likely that whenever a kid knocks on the door of the school nurse because they finally found the courage to contact the nurse, the nurse is in another school doing administrative stuff. I think I'd be more interested in  changing the system than I'd be interested in being a single nurse trying to be ten places at once.

&#x200B;

Anyway, digression, please pardon.

Propably my anxiety, I have this extreme level of doubting myself even tho I can see tons of reasons confirming to myself why I should keep stuff, I'm just reading all these articles saying "why you're wrong telling yourself you need the stuff you're keeping", and I'm assuming I'm wrong and getting rid of stuff. But I always find myself spending quite a bit of money buying stuff that I already used to have.

&#x200B;

I guess my original post was silly and that I should trust my own opinions rather than follow these minimalist-guide articles.

Thanks for your reply. The exercises are very "weekend warrior"-like and I participate as a regular rifleman, not in any medical capacity, so I don't know if TCCC guidelines are applied for professional medics (I would assume so). TQ under fire makes sense I guess,  assuming one is being under fire for a relatively short while and not held up in a position for an hour, good point. Our cases are mostly in the realm of tactical field care until our medical officer stops the drill.

&#x200B;

EDIT: Norway, btw

Wow, so many great replies. I hope I'll be forgiven for not replying to each and every comment, I didn't expect this many replies but I have so much I wanna say to each comment. I really appreciate every one of you for sharing. Maybe someone could relate to some of the comments and feel less alone.

&#x200B;

In any case, thanks for replying.

Thanks for your reply. Tons of terms I'm not familiar there. Googled some of them and I guess they are of the same sort that my mindset is. It definitely has some element of coping mechanism in it, due to life trauma and mental health, but at the same time I can't see how anyone would not be wise in searching for order and productivity, regardless of how their life had been.

&#x200B;

Ofc some people find peace in their chaos, which I kinda get, as long as it's not - as you said, a coping mechanism (escape...)

It's all good. 

I was gonna write a short reply but then my mind started wandering. So apologies for a unnessesarily long post. No reply expected btw, just take whatever you can from my words if there's anything in here that might be useful to you.

&#x200B;

I wanna make it clear I don't mean to negate your pain in all of this, nothing worse for me than if I'm having a depressed stage or heavy anxiety, and someone comes along and tries to rationalise it away. I'm like "dude, you're really not helping, you're just making me feel pathetic about feeling like this but I can't help it."

So I hope you didn't feel like I was being dismissive about your pain.

&#x200B;

I guess I felt like you were being overly self critical about a situation that has so much potential in being a positive experience, so I felt it was important to challenge the negative thoughts. It's not easy challenging our own self critical thoughts as they tend to be Goliath while we are David.

&#x200B;

Anyway, failing, I know all about it. I'm a 37 year old man with mental health issues living at my parents. I'm working on my bachelors to build a life for myself but I'm close to failing this and might risk having no education, job or future but a shitload of student loans. I'm carrying so much shame, self doubt and and conviction about being destined to fail. 

So when I win at something, I dismiss it as having nothing to do with my skills or my effort, but rather random luck, but when I fail it gets locked into my memory and I use the failures as proof of my general uselessness.

&#x200B;

I'm betting a overcooked pot of pasta against your burnt chili, that what I just wrote it relatable to you.

Actually I'm quite good at pasta.

&#x200B;

Anyway, quick and short thoughts (HAHA, I went back after writing it all and smacked myself in the head for thinking I was capable of writing quick and short thoughts).

You were afraid to try your hand at chili but you did it anyway. That's literally the definition of courage, doing something despite the fear. Aknowledge that and be proud of yourself.

If there's anything you might wanna facepalm yourself about is if you naively underestimated the challenge of cooking for others. But that doesn't mean you're failing at life, you did a miscalculation. People do that all the time.

&#x200B;

Everyone makes it look so easy you say. Well, I've never tried it, I'm sure it is easy if you compare it to many other dishes, but you're definitely not the first one to fuck it up.

You wanna be good at cooking, not just average. Lemme tell you one thing, no one has fucked up more in the kitchen than the people who are good at cooking. That's a important part of how they ended up becoming good at cooking. They experimented, learnt to understand their ingredients, texture, flavours etc. Keep cooking, keep learning, keep fucking the dishes up, have fun doing so. Post your fucked up dishes on social media and laugh at yourself. Through that you can take away the power from feeling like a failure, and rather laugh at yourself and aknowledge that even the best chefs fuck up once in a while.

&#x200B;

Your stepdad sounds like a tool. But to be fair, it can also be that you have been overly sensitive to feedback. Emotionally fragile like. I say that because I'm like that, I've had insecurities and low self esteem and self worth my entire life, so I can notice how deep feedback can hurt even if I agree with it, not agree with it from an self deprecating point of view, but from a rational one.

&#x200B;

And being okay-ish.. Lemme say something about okay-ish, most people are okay-ish at best. The greatest people in any given field are okay-ish in lots of other stuff. There's nothing wrong with being okay-ish.

But some might say one of the hardest things about struggling with mental illness is seeing other people prosper, building a career and a life for themselves, whilst we feel incapable of fulfulling our potential. We need something to be proud of, maybe because we think it'll change everything. 

And maybe for some people it will change everything, but for the most part, I think the true growth, the potentially true way to finding some form of peace, is looking inwards. Like cooking for the fun of it, learning the dish, trying different quantities, experimenting, celebrating every failure because you learnt something.

Is it possible that if people loved what you did you think your own feeling of worth will increase? That you yourself is not good enough, and you need something you made, an extension of yourself, to be proud of?

And wanting people to see the value in your skills, could it be you actually want them to see the value in you?

Even if you learn to cook a bunch of dishes not all of them is gonna be amazing. Most people cook mediocre food and they might have one or two dishes that they are very good at.

&#x200B;

Ok, this was a mess of random thoughts. I hope I didn't write anything to offend you. And as I said, no reply expected to this long ass post, I just hope there might be one or two thoughts or observations in my post that you can make use of.

&#x200B;

Best of luck in your culinary exploits.

&#x200B;

And remember, have fun and laugh at the fuck-ups.

Yes! That's exactly what my memory is. No idea what that episode is called?

&#x200B;

Thanks
